#!/usr/bin/env python3
# aos-daemon-ctl - 把 proc 掛到 daemon、起停睡醒暫停、自檢、清理舊列、門鈴、停 daemon 本身與叫清道夫跑一輪（sweep，本體 py/aos_sweep）；說明在 cmd/docs/03、04。
HELP = """aos-daemon-ctl - attach procs to an aos-daemon and start / stop / sleep / wake / pause them

usage: aos-daemon-ctl [--daemon D] SUBCOMMAND [FLAGS...] [ARGS...]
       aos-daemon-ctl SUBCOMMAND --help

stopping and starting again (three pairs; start / wake / resume only undo their own pair):
  what you want            command   how it stops                 table after  back with  daemon restart
  finish this round, stop  stop      writes its ctl, waits        stopped      start      stays stopped
  stop right now           sleep     SIGTERM (SIGKILL after 10 s) sleeping     wake       wakes it
  hold in place            pause     writes its ctl; still alive  running      resume     -
  take it off the table    rm        stop, then remove the row    (no row)     add again  -
  the whole daemon         shutdown  every proc goes to sleep     sleeping     restart    all wake

subcommands:
  add [FLAGS] XXX [-- ARGS...]   attach (register + start); prints the letter name
  rm NAME        detach (stop, then remove; stack kept, so it does not ask)
  prune [--older-than DUR] [--apply]   check rows; preview or detach old stopped / sleeping rows
  stop NAME      write its public ctl and wait for exit (row stays as stopped)
  start NAME     start a stopped one again on the same stack
  sleep NAME     SIGTERM now (running stays sleeping; stopping stays stopped)
  wake NAME      start a sleeping one again on the same stack
  pause NAME     write {"cmd": "pause", "seq": N} to its ctl window; prints N
  resume NAME    the same with "resume"
  ls [-j]        the table: NAME  PHASE  PID  CODE  WS  REASON; last line = the supervisor's view
  where --stack NAME   print its stack (one absolute path)
  poke [--ctl|--mail|--all]   wake now: read ctl (default), ctl + mailbox, or run a full round early
  ping [--timeout-s S]   wait for the daemon's ping reply; prints "replied in N ms"

daemon itself:
  shutdown [--timeout-s S]   stop this daemon: write stop to its own ctl, poke, wait for exit; prints one line saying so

sweeper (the same code as the _sweeper row, aos-sweeper):
  sweep [--dry-run]           one round now: archive big stderr files, stop orphaned loops, report
  sweep --purge [--dry-run]   delete every stderr archive of this daemon

flag order: -d may go anywhere, also after the subcommand and its NAME (stop ann -d D);
a subcommand's flags may go before or after its NAME / XXX. After -- (add XXX -- ARGS)
nothing is read as a flag.

Mutation commands drop letters into the daemon's ctl mailbox (procs_added, procs_removed,
procs_stopped, ...) or write the proc's own ctl window (pause / resume). A letter only
asks; the daemon applies it on its next round. See the result with ls.
Letters always go straight to this daemon's ctl mailbox; AOS_KERNEL is not read.
To go through the kernel (AOS_KERNEL), use aos-agent ctl instead.
add / rm / stop / start / sleep / wake also take --reply-to BOX (send only, do not wait).
Without --reply-to they read the daemon's reply: a refused letter (bad body) is an error
with the daemon's code and reason (BodyBad).

flags:
  -d, --daemon D   the daemon's stack folder (default: $AOS_DAEMON, else ./.stack)
  -h, --help       show this help
  short names: -n --name, -w --ws, -s --stack, -j --json

exit codes: 0 sent / printed; 1 error ("aos-daemon-ctl: error: CODE reason" on stderr).
more: proto8/cmd/docs/03-aos-daemon-ctl.md
"""

HELPS = {
    "poke": """aos-daemon-ctl poke - wake the daemon now

usage: aos-daemon-ctl [--daemon D] poke [--ctl|--mail|--all]

--ctl (default) reads ctl and reconciles procs, without mailbox or body work.
--mail also handles the mailbox, without running the body.
--all runs a full round early, including the body even before its next scheduled run.
Choose at most one mode; no positional arguments are accepted.

This is a doorbell: first write ctl with another command or yourself, or send a letter,
then poke. Repeated pokes merge into the strongest mode. A running body is not
interrupted; the daemon checks queued pokes as soon as the body finishes.

The state window's pid, started_at_ticks and boot_id must match /proc before a
signal is sent through pidfd; otherwise DaemonMissing. The daemon must declare its
poke signals in that window. Cross-account or sandbox permission denial is PokeFailed.

exit codes: 0 sent (no output); 1 error (ArgumentsBad, DaemonMissing, StateMissing,
  StateBad, PokeFailed).
