XXX follows the aos-exec entry chain: an object with exec and no argv supplies a loop
config (no ARGS allowed); arrays and argv objects stay unchanged as aos-exec targets.
Launch syntax only selects how to start a proc; all procs share lifecycle rules. Prints the
letter name, then waits up to --timeout-s seconds for the letter to leave new/ (still there = a
warning line, exit 0; it is applied when taken). A refused letter (bad body) = error BodyBad.

A definition folder runs its _entry, as aos-exec would. An agent folder's _entry attaches the
agent itself and can fail with a name collision (left stopped with a reason); to attach an agent, use
aos-agent ctl add, or give its body: add -n bob --def bob bob/body.json.
"applied": true only means the letter was applied to the table, not that the proc still runs.

Paths on the table: one you type as an absolute path (XXX, --ws, --stack, --def) stays that
absolute path (the letter carries it in "as_written"); a relative one is taken from the current
directory and stored relative to the daemon's stack, so moving the whole tree keeps working.
A JSON string given to --ws-json or --stack-json goes into the letter as written (a relative
one counts from the daemon's cwd); one given to --path-json is read like XXX.

